  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transboundary Rivers of South Asia (TROSA) Phase -2</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The goal of this 2nd phase of Transboundary Rivers of South Asia (TROSA) is to strengthen resilience to climate change and environmental and natural disasters of riparian communities in the transboundary Ganges Brahmaputra Meghna (GBM) river basins. The project will work on the following outcomes - 1. Strengthened climate -resilient livelihoods of communities living in the transboundary GBM river basins. 2. Improved and inclusive management of transboundary river ecosystems across the GBM river basins. 3. Strengthened leadership of civil society, especially women, Indigenous People, and youth to influence government and private sector on water governance across and between the transboundary GBM basins. 4. Strengthened cooperation, collaboration, and accountability across and between the transboundary GBM river basins.  Through the Inception phase, Oxfam in Bangladesh intends to cover - 1. Engagement and participation of all level stakeholders including riparian grassroots communities (fisherfolks, farmers, indigenous community, day -labourer, etc.), government and non -government entities, researchers, academia, youth, private sectors, Civil Society Organisations (CSOs), Community Based Organisations (CBOs), etc. 2. Dissemination of learning of TROSA Phase 1 and introduction of T2:3R. 3. Create a network including river governance actors and influential. 4. Understand the basin -wise context within the climate change -river governance nexus through the wider -group perception as a community from Haor and Char, lower Ganges (Padma) are now engaged. 5. Understanding the impact where more than two major rivers join (Meghna and Padma). 6. Monitor gender segregation in different sectors of river management. 7. Scoping out potential local Non Governmental Organisations (NGOs), researchers, and other organizations/individuals for future collaboration. 8. Understanding the setup of the existing practice of Nature -based Solutions and scoping for future riverine ecosystems management</narrative>
  </description>
